Luminance helper:
```cpp
static bool IsDarkColor(COLORREF color)
{
    const int luminance = (GetRValue(color) * 299 + GetGValue(color) * 587 + GetBValue(color) * 114) / 1000;
    return luminance < 128;
}
```
This is a genuine non-obvious invariant worth a comment.

`NP_API BOOL SetThemeMode(HWND hPanel, int mode)`:
```cpp
NP_API BOOL SetThemeMode(HWND hPanel, int mode)
{
    NotepadInstance *inst = GetInstance(hPanel);
    if (!inst || mode < NP_THEME_SYSTEM || mode > NP_THEME_DARK)
        return FALSE;
    inst->themeMode = (ThemeMode)mode;
    inst->customTheme = false;
    inst->darkMode = ResolveDarkMode(inst->themeMode);
    inst->theme = GetPalette(inst->darkMode);
    ApplyThemeToDocuments(inst);
    if (inst->hSelf) InvalidateRect(...);
    FireNotify(inst, NP_NOTIFY_THEME_CHANGED, -1);
    return TRUE;
}

NP_API int GetThemeMode(HWND hPanel)
{
    NotepadInstance *inst = GetInstance(hPanel);
    if (!inst) return NP_THEME_DARK;
    return (int)inst->themeMode;
}
```

Note: `NP_THEME_SYSTEM` etc. must be added to NotepadPanel.h first.

Also `SetTheme(NULL)` should reset customTheme and use mode palette. And GetTheme returns the current palette.

Potential issue: `SetThemeMode` with System + custom theme applied → the palette resets to the mode palette. That's expected.

Now also: the panel should react to `WM_SETTINGCHANGE` when `themeMode == System` (for the standalone app). Add to PanelWndProc:
```cpp
case WM_SETTINGCHANGE:
    if (inst && inst->themeMode == ThemeMode::System && lParam &&
        wcscmp((LPCWSTR)lParam, L"ImmersiveColorSet") == 0)
    {
        ApplyThemeMode(inst, ThemeMode::System);
    }
    return 0;
```
Hmm, but the standalone app also re-themes its frame. It can handle its own WM_SETTINGCHANGE. The panel can handle its own for embedded use. Both are fine (both receive it; the panel is a child window, and WM_SETTINGCHANGE is broadcast to top-level windows only, not children!). So the panel won't receive it when embedded. For the standalone app, the frame gets it and can call `SetThemeMode` again — but that would only work if the mode is System.

